📘 **dplyr Exam-Focused Short Notes**

In dplyr, the most important idea is: manipulate data using simple verbs.

🔹 **Core functions (must remember)**
`filter()` → keep rows (condition must be TRUE)
`select()` → choose columns
`mutate()` → create/modify columns
`summarise()` → reduce data (mean, sum, etc.)
`group_by()` → group data
`arrange()` → sort data (`desc()` for descending)

🔹 **Very common pattern (EXAM IMPORTANT)**
👉 `group_by()` + `summarise()`
Used for averages, totals, counts
Example: average per group

🔹 **Top / Bottom values**
Use `slice_max()` / `slice_min()`
OR `arrange(desc(x))`

🔹 **Ranking (IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE)**
`min_rank()` → ties, gaps
`dense_rank()` → ties, no gaps
`row_number()` → no ties

👉 Use: `min_rank(desc(x))` for highest = rank 1

🔹 **Conditions in filter()**
`>`, `<`, `==`, `!=`
`&` (AND), `|` (OR)
`%in%` (multiple values)
`is.na()` (missing values)

🔹 **Handling missing values**
Always use: `na.rm = TRUE` in `mean()`, `sum()`

🔹 **Pipes (VERY IMPORTANT)**
`%>%` → passes result forward
Makes code readable step-by-step

🔹 **Joins (basic idea)**
`left_join(x, y)` → keep all rows from x
`inner_join(x, y)` → keep matching rows only

🔹 **New shortcut**
`.by` → grouping without `group_by()`

🔹 **Common mistakes (EXAM TRAPS)**
❌ `=` instead of `==` in filter
❌ forgetting `na.rm = TRUE`
❌ using `desc()` inside `mutate()` alone
❌ ranking before summarising

🔹 **Golden Rule**
👉 First `group_by()` → then `summarise()` → then `arrange()` or `rank`

🎯 **One-line memory**
Filter rows, select columns, mutate new data, group and summarise, then arrange.

